! Silicon in a sheared cell: the case whose stress tensor is not diagonal.
!
! Every other stress reference here has a cell with enough symmetry that the
! shear components are zero or nearly so, and a sign error or a transposed
! deformation in `Calculation.at_strain` is invisible in such a tensor -- it
! moves the diagonal by nothing and the off-diagonal from zero to zero. This
! cell is the fcc primitive one with `a3` tilted by 4% in the xy plane, given
! explicitly with `ibrav = 0`, which leaves the crystal with no symmetry
! operation but the identity and every entry of `sigma` free.
!
! Two things follow from `nosym` being effectively true here and both are the
! point of the case: the tensor is compared to QE *unsymmetrised*, so nothing
! averages a mistake away, and the finite-difference check in
! tests/regression/test_stress.py can strain this cell along any of the six
! independent directions without leaving the group it was set up with.
!
! The atom is left on its ideal site: what is being tested is the cell, and a
! displacement would mix the two.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tstress = .true.
    tprnfor = .true.
    verbosity = 'high'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=0, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
CELL_PARAMETERS (alat)
  -0.50   0.00   0.50
   0.00   0.50   0.50
  -0.54   0.46   0.00
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 0 0 0
